These are the challenges faced by Telia:
- Climate and energy use
- E-waste and circularity
- Point 3
- Point 4
- Point 5

*Climate and energy use.* Telecom networks and data centres need a lot of electricity, so the industry is closely connected to SDG 7. SDG 7 includes targets about increasing the share of renewable energy and improving energy efficiency faster by 2030 (United Nations, n.d.-a). In the EU, telecom networks used around 1–1.2% of all electricity in 2022 (Kamiya & Bertoldi, 2024). 5G is expected to be more energy efficient than 4G, but since the networks get denser it is still unclear how much energy it will use in total (International Energy Agency, n.d.). Engwall et al. (2017, pp. 438–439) mention efficient use of energy as part of environmental responsibility. Since Telia runs networks in several Nordic and Baltic countries, how it powers and builds out these networks matters for the goals.

*E-waste and circularity.* As a mobile operator, Telia sells phones together with subscriptions, so what happens to the devices afterwards is a sustainability issue for the company and connects to SDG 12. Target 12.5 says that waste should be reduced substantially by 2030 through prevention, reduction, recycling and reuse (United Nations, n.d.-b). In 2022 the world produced a record 62 billion kg of e-waste, and only 22.3% was documented as properly collected and recycled (ITU & UNITAR, 2024). About 95% of a new smartphone's emissions in its first year come from manufacturing it (Lee, 2021), so replacing phones often is bad for the climate. Engwall et al. (2017, p. 443) describe the circular economy as a model where waste is seen as a raw material instead of something to throw away. Telia therefore needs to work with reuse, refurbishment and recycling of devices.

